## 5. Judging the bare literal `30` — the builder's reasoning holds

The builder kept `seen === 30` as a bare literal and argued that (a) deriving
it from anything recomputable from the same source is blind to the same regex
gap, and (b) an independent expected-list duplicates the source of truth.

(a) is correct and is the decisive half. Any derived expectation — "count the
banner lines that have a backing constant" — walks the same
`extractUsageConstants`, so a constant made unparseable lowers BOTH sides and
the assertion becomes vacuous. That is precisely the `sawAny` failure mode
wearing a number. The literal is the only value that does not move when the
scanner's coverage does. I accept the reasoning.

(b) is the weaker half, and N2 is its ceiling: the count is a scalar, so a
pair of edits that cancel (one constant drops out, one line added elsewhere)
holds it at 30. A pinned SET of the 30 `file:CONST:line` identities would
catch N2 and would not duplicate the usage TEXT (only the inventory of which
constants exist). I am not requiring it:
  - N2 needs two coordinated edits that happen to cancel exactly; N1 — the
    realistic single regression, and the actual historical one — is caught;
  - the QA lex §2 is explicit that one mutation demonstrating the suite bites
    is enough to pass review, and eight of mine agree that it bites;
  - the constant's own comment already states the ceiling honestly ("30 is
    exact for this tree today; it is meant to move only when a deliberate
    edit ... moves it, in the same commit").
Recorded as a known ceiling, not a defect. If a future opus touches this
guard, the set-of-identities form is the upgrade.

