## 9. Verdict

PASS — round 3.

What convinces me, specifically:

  - I ran `node scripts/w030-usage-guard-mutation.mjs` in an isolated
    worktree at b47bb43 and watched the pin fail at 6 with exit 1, then
    watched `git status --porcelain` come back empty. The committed red is
    reproducible, and it is a red the tool could not have recorded had the
    assertion not genuinely failed.
  - I wrote eight of my own mutations and the pin caught seven. It fails on
    ONE constant dropping out (29), which the old `sawAny` did not; it fails
    on inflation (31), so the count cannot be padded; it fails on a banner
    line deleted underneath a constant. The one survivor, N2, needs two
    edits that cancel exactly, and its cheaper upgrade path is written down
    in §5.
  - I recomputed 0c5e9389bc12d47c549bd49c9d64449030d47a0c by hand from
    `git ls-tree -r b47bb43` with the manifest's own exclude set, and all
    three automated gates certify that exact hash. I then re-ran all three
    commands and got their recorded exit codes.
  - `git merge-tree` is clean, master's round-2 verdict survives in the
    branch's record, and `check` on the merge preview has two FEWER blocking
    findings than the branch's base and none naming W-030.
  - I built the merge preview and ran the full suite on it: 1173 PASS, 0
    FAIL, pin still exactly 30.

Round 1: the evidence did not say what it claimed. Round 2: the evidence was
true but the guard could be hollowed out silently. Round 3: the guard fails
on a single missing constant, and I proved it by removing one.

DO NOT MERGE on this verdict alone — §8's two open items (certificate
staleness against today's master, and where this verdict lands) are the
integrator's, and W-033/D-015 own them.

